{"conjecture": "JE = KP_y, i.e. segment EJ has the same length as segment KP_y, for every generic P_x on h1.", "reason": "Partly derived, partly checked only on the given coordinates. (1) j passes through F on the diameter GF and is parallel to the tangent f at G, so j is perpendicular to GF: j is the tangent at F. Then e is parallel to GF, and h1 is perpendicular to j. (2) Projecting onto the direction of j gives proj(V−F) ≈ 2.309 and proj(K−F) ≈ 4.619 = 2·proj(V−F). This is a numerical check, not a proof. It indicates that e is the reflection of the focus F across h1. So F is a focus, h1 is the tangent at the vertex and e is the directrix of a parabola with axis j. (3) By the classical pedal property, the line m, perpendicular to FP_x at P_x on the vertex tangent, is tangent to that parabola. Its contact point is where m meets the line through the directrix point K parallel to the axis j. So P_y is on the parabola and P_yK = P_yF, a focus–directrix distance. (4) H is the foot of P_y on j, and J lies on the tangent g with HJ parallel to GE. The tangent lengths VE = VF and the parallel HJ ∥ DE give VJ/VE = VH/VD. I did not complete a symbolic proof that this transfers P_yF to EJ. (5) Numerically, |EJ|² = 4.4328² + 10.4431² ≈ 128.71 and |KP_y|² = 11.2604² + 1.3826² ≈ 128.71, so EJ = KP_y ≈ 11.345. The non-collinearity of E, J, K and the absence of a trapezoid among the four points were also checked numerically.", "closes_goal": true}
